{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## How Cloud's readers actually page (established from the code, not assumed)\n\nAll three readers in adapters/jira/acli_graph.py (get_parent_map, get_comment_map,\nget_issuelinks_map) drive POST /rest/api/3/search/jql with an OPAQUE cursor:\nthe request body carries {jql, maxResults: 100, fields: [<one field>]}, plus\nnextPageToken echoed back from the previous response. Termination is\n\"if resp.get('isLast'): break\" then \"if not next_page_token: break\".\n\nThis is structurally DIFFERENT from the DC bug family. DC advanced an integer offset by\nthe REQUESTED page size, so a server that capped pages below the request (a lowered\njira.search.views.default.max) made a short first page read as \"that is all there is\".\nCloud never computes an offset; it hands back whatever token the server gave. So the\nadvance-by-requested-size arithmetic does not exist here to get wrong.\n\nMeasured on the branch before any test was written (stub over _direct_rest_post_json,\n250 issues):\n  * 250 seeded at 100/page -> 250 recovered in 3 requests, cursors sent\n    [None, cursor:100, cursor:200]\n  * pages capped at 40 while 100 requested (the DC-shaped short-page server)\n    -> 250 recovered in 7 requests; a SHORT page does NOT stop the walk\n  * final page with no isLast and no token -> 250 recovered in 3 requests\n\nSo Cloud pages CORRECTLY today. Per the ticket's own framing, the deliverable is\ntherefore a net that would CATCH a regression, not a bug report -- with one exception\nrecorded below.\n\n## Seeding, and why\n\n250 items at 100/page = THREE pages. Two pages can pass a fencepost error by accident;\nthree cannot. _SEEDED_ISSUES=250, _EXPECTED_PAGE_SIZE=100, _EXPECTED_PAGES=3 are\nmodule-local LITERALS, deliberately not imported from the reader's own page_size -- an\nexpectation derived from the constant under test moves with the bug and can never fail.\nThe short-page variant seeds the same 250 against a 40-item server cap = 7 pages.\nAssertions are on the recovered SET (plus payload identity spot-checks at index 0, 105\nand 249), not on a count, so a reader returning the right NUMBER of wrong items fails.\n\n## New test module\n\ntests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_acli_graph_pagination_heldout.py (521 lines, new file;\nadapters/jira/acli_graph.py unchanged at 606 lines -- NO production change in this\nchange). 27 passed + 1 xfailed. Sits beside the DC equivalent\ntest_dc_transport_pagination_heldout.py for parity.\n\nAC coverage:\n  * AC1 recovered set + payload identity across 3 pages, per reader\n  * AC2 cursor advance asserted on the recorded request BODIES: request 0 must carry NO\n    token; request N must equal the token response N-1 returned; no token re-sent\n  * AC3 both stop conditions in ISOLATION -- isLast alone (the final page deliberately\n    still offers a live token, so a token-only reader would spin) and an exhausted cursor\n    alone (no isLast; both the absent-token and explicit-null spellings)\n  * AC4 strict xfail, see the defect section below\n  * AC5 mutation ledger below\n  * AC6 an AST guard over adapters/jira/*.py: any function POSTing to\n    /rest/api/3/search/jql must both loop and mention nextPageToken. DC's guard scans the\n    DC transport source only, so this seam was open on Cloud; both together close the\n    class on EITHER deployment. The guard shares its predicate with a teeth test driving\n    a synthetic offender (with a paginated reader and a non-search POST as negative\n    controls) and with a coverage test that fails if the scan ever goes vacuous."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785720499073841001,"uuid":"c51c4b4e-278e-4e04-9d67-d0f39fd16be0"}